#version 460
// PHOTON R5 — THE SPATIAL FILTER, and the composite that follows it (round C).
//
// WHY THERE IS A SECOND PASS AT ALL. One ray per pixel per frame is an
// unbiased estimator of the specular lobe and a TERRIBLE one: its variance is
// the lobe's own, and the temporal mean reduces it only as fast as the history
// is allowed to remember. Round B measured exactly that — with the roughness
// decode corrected, a surface at perceptual 0.3 left 5/255 of frame-to-frame
// movement at the 99th percentile after sixteen frames, against a bar of 2 —
// and named the answer: a SPATIAL filter, which is what every shipping
// implementation of this uses, because neighbouring pixels of a rough surface
// are samples of very nearly the same integral and averaging them costs
// nothing in bias.
//
// WHAT IT IS. One à-trous / bilateral pass at the trace's own resolution over
// the temporal mean, guided by three things and nothing else:
//
//   * THE LOBE. A MIRROR IS NOT FILTERED AT ALL — radius 0 at or below
//     kMirrorAlpha. There is no variance to remove there (every sample is the
//     same ray) and any blur would be a real loss of a real image: a mirror
//     shows the world at the world's own sharpness. The radius grows with
//     sqrt(alpha), i.e. with the PERCEPTUAL roughness, because that is what the
//     reflected lobe's angular width goes as — the blur tracks the blur the
//     surface itself is applying.
//   * DEPTH. A tap on the other side of a silhouette is a different surface and
//     its reflection is a different integral; the weight falls off with the
//     relative depth difference, so the filter never leaks across an edge.
//   * THE NORMAL. Two texels of the same wall at different orientations reflect
//     different directions. The weight is a power of the cosine between them.
//
// AND IT ALSO DOES THE COMPOSITE, for one reason: the composite has to read the
// FILTERED mean, and a third dispatch to move one value would cost another
// full-resolution read of everything the composite already has in registers.
//
// SAME DESCRIPTOR SET as rq_reflect.comp — the same layout, the same bound set,
// the same Params — so the second pipeline costs a module and a bind, not a
// second set of plumbing.
// The acceleration-structure BINDING is declared below purely to keep one
// descriptor set layout for both pipelines, so the extension has to be here
// even though this shader traces nothing.
#extension GL_EXT_ray_query : require

layout( local_size_x = 8, local_size_y = 8, local_size_z = 1 ) in;

/// Must match rq_reflect.comp.
const int kMaxCascades = 4;

/// The acceleration structure is in the shared layout and is NOT used here; a
/// binding may be declared and unread, and declaring it is what keeps one
/// layout for two pipelines.
layout( set = 0, binding = 0 ) uniform accelerationStructureEXT tlas;

layout( set = 0, binding = 1 ) uniform Params
{
	/// xyz = the camera in world space, w = 1 for a perspective camera and 0
	/// for an orthographic one (the reconstruction differs — see worldAt()).
	vec4 camPos;
	/// The world-space ray basis: worldDir(u,v) = rayTL + rayRight*u + rayDown*v,
	/// scaled so its component along the camera's forward axis is exactly 1.
	/// Then `camPos + worldDir * viewDistance` is the surface. Under an
	/// orthographic camera rayTL/rayRight/rayDown carry the image PLANE instead
	/// (an offset, not a ray) and `fwd` is the one view direction.
	vec4 rayTL;
	vec4 rayRight;
	vec4 rayDown;
	vec4 fwd;
	/// x = A, y = B of Camera::getProjectionParamsAB() (reverse-Z safe),
	/// z = the far plane (the orthographic depth scale), w = the roughness
	/// gate's FEATHER half-width (kRayReflectFeather).
	vec4 projParams;
	/// xy = the trace resolution in pixels, zw = the full-resolution size of
	/// jahSsrReflection.
	vec4 resolution;
	/// x = the roughness gate — the PROJECT's dial, not a renderer constant:
	/// above it the probe/field photograph is a good enough integral of a wide
	/// lobe and a ray is spent for nothing. The transition is FEATHERED over
	/// projParams.w either side of it. y = the ray's maximum length in
	/// world units, z = the frame index (the sample sequence's only input),
	/// w = how many cascades are bound.
	vec4 knobs;
	/// x = 1 when the voxel textures carry the anisotropic slots, y = the
	/// surface bias in world units (a ray starts this far off its own surface),
	/// z = 1 when a sky cubemap is bound, w = the history's minimum blend
	/// weight (the floor on 1/n — how long the temporal mean remembers).
	vec4 knobs2;
	/// The colour an escaping ray reads when no sky cubemap is bound.
	vec4 skyColour;
	/// The VIEW space's three axes in world space (the camera's orientation as
	/// a rotation): what turns the G-buffer's view-space normal back into one.
	vec4 viewAxisX;
	vec4 viewAxisY;
	vec4 viewAxisZ;
	/// Per cascade: xyz = the voxel box's world origin, w = the box's largest
	/// side in world units (the cell size times the resolution).
	vec4 voxelOrigin[kMaxCascades];
	/// Per cascade: xyz = 1 / the voxel box's world size, w = one cell in world
	/// units (the sample point's bias along the ray).
	vec4 voxelInvSize[kMaxCascades];
	/// THE PREVIOUS FRAME'S CAMERA, in exactly the same five numbers as this
	/// frame's (camPos, rayTL, rayRight, rayDown, fwd). Not a view-projection
	/// matrix, deliberately: inverting the basis above is the same arithmetic
	/// forwards and backwards, so the reprojection cannot disagree with the
	/// reconstruction about a handedness, a y-flip or a depth convention — and
	/// each of those is a silent half-pixel-to-whole-image error that only shows
	/// up as a smear while the camera moves.
	vec4 prevCamPos;
	vec4 prevRayTL;
	vec4 prevRayRight;
	vec4 prevRayDown;
	vec4 prevFwd;
	/// ---- THE SECOND EYE (lane REFLECT-VR-1) -----------------------------
	/// x = 1 when this target carries TWO EYES SIDE BY SIDE — the left eye in
	/// the left half of every texture here, the right in the right half
	/// (chain::applyStereo's viewport split). Then the five basis vectors above
	/// are the LEFT eye's and the five below are the right eye's, each mapped
	/// over its OWN half as a 0..1 image; with x = 0 the block below is unread
	/// and the basis above is the one camera's, exactly as before.
	///
	/// WHY A HALF TEST AND NOT TWO DISPATCHES: the depth, the normals and the
	/// roughness of BOTH eyes live in one texture at one scale, and the pixel's
	/// own coordinate already selects the right eye's texels. Only the RAY
	/// needs to know which eye it belongs to — one branch on the x coordinate,
	/// no second descriptor set, no second history ring, and the seam falls on
	/// a block boundary by construction (the C++ side sizes the trace so).
	vec4 stereo;
	vec4 camPos2;
	vec4 rayTL2;
	vec4 rayRight2;
	vec4 rayDown2;
	vec4 fwd2;
	vec4 prevCamPos2;
	vec4 prevRayTL2;
	vec4 prevRayRight2;
	vec4 prevRayDown2;
	vec4 prevFwd2;
} p;

/// ONE EYE'S IMAGE, as the five numbers everything below needs: where it is,
/// and the world-space ray (or image-plane offset) of its own 0..1 image.
/// @see p.stereo — with one eye there is one of these and nothing branches.
struct EyeImage
{
	vec3  camPos;
	float perspective;		// 1 = a ray per pixel, 0 = an offset (orthographic)
	vec3  rayTL;
	vec3  rayRight;
	vec3  rayDown;
	vec3  fwd;
};

EyeImage eyeNow( int eye )
{
	EyeImage e;
	if( eye == 1 )
	{
		e.camPos = p.camPos2.xyz; e.perspective = p.camPos2.w;
		e.rayTL = p.rayTL2.xyz; e.rayRight = p.rayRight2.xyz;
		e.rayDown = p.rayDown2.xyz; e.fwd = p.fwd2.xyz;
	}
	else
	{
		e.camPos = p.camPos.xyz; e.perspective = p.camPos.w;
		e.rayTL = p.rayTL.xyz; e.rayRight = p.rayRight.xyz;
		e.rayDown = p.rayDown.xyz; e.fwd = p.fwd.xyz;
	}
	return e;
}

EyeImage eyeBefore( int eye )
{
	EyeImage e;
	if( eye == 1 )
	{
		e.camPos = p.prevCamPos2.xyz; e.perspective = p.prevCamPos2.w;
		e.rayTL = p.prevRayTL2.xyz; e.rayRight = p.prevRayRight2.xyz;
		e.rayDown = p.prevRayDown2.xyz; e.fwd = p.prevFwd2.xyz;
	}
	else
	{
		e.camPos = p.prevCamPos.xyz; e.perspective = p.prevCamPos.w;
		e.rayTL = p.prevRayTL.xyz; e.rayRight = p.prevRayRight.xyz;
		e.rayDown = p.prevRayDown.xyz; e.fwd = p.prevFwd.xyz;
	}
	return e;
}

/// WHICH EYE OWNS A COLUMN, and the whole of the stereo layout in one place:
/// the left eye is the left half of the image, the right eye the right half.
int eyeOfColumn( int x, int width )
{
	return ( p.stereo.x > 0.5 && x >= width / 2 ) ? 1 : 0;
}
/// That eye's own 0..1 image coordinate from a coordinate over the whole
/// target. Horizontally it is the half's own fraction; vertically nothing is
/// split, so it passes straight through.
vec2 eyeLocalUv( vec2 uv, int eye )
{
	if( p.stereo.x <= 0.5 )
		return uv;
	return vec2( clamp( uv.x * 2.0 - float( eye ), 0.0, 1.0 ), uv.y );
}
/// ...and back: a coordinate in the eye's own image, as one over the target.
vec2 eyeGlobalUv( vec2 local, int eye )
{
	if( p.stereo.x <= 0.5 )
		return local;
	return vec2( ( local.x + float( eye ) ) * 0.5, local.y );
}

layout( set = 0, binding = 2 ) uniform sampler2D gBufNormals;
layout( set = 0, binding = 3 ) uniform sampler2D gBufShadowRoughness;
layout( set = 0, binding = 4 ) uniform sampler2D sceneDepth;
layout( rgba16f, set = 0, binding = 5 ) uniform image2D ssrReflection;
/// THE TEMPORAL MEAN, PING-PONGED. Two buffers and not one, because a pixel
/// reads the mean at its own REPROJECTED coordinate — any pixel of the buffer —
/// while its neighbours are writing theirs. One buffer makes that a read/write
/// race within a single dispatch (the value read would be this frame's or last
/// frame's depending on the scheduler), and a temporal filter fed by a race is
/// how a stable picture acquires a shimmer nobody can reproduce. The pair costs
/// one extra image of 8 bytes per traced pixel.
layout( rgba16f, set = 0, binding = 6 ) uniform readonly  image2D historyPrev;
layout( rgba16f, set = 0, binding = 7 ) uniform image2D historyCur;
/// ...and the distance beside it, which is what makes the reprojection a TEST
/// rather than a hope: the surface found at the reprojected pixel is the same
/// surface only if it was the same distance away.
layout( r32f, set = 0, binding = 8 ) uniform readonly  image2D historyDistPrev;
layout( r32f, set = 0, binding = 9 ) uniform image2D historyDistCur;
layout( set = 0, binding = 10 ) uniform sampler3D voxelIso[kMaxCascades];
layout( set = 0, binding = 11 ) uniform sampler3D voxelX[kMaxCascades];
layout( set = 0, binding = 12 ) uniform sampler3D voxelY[kMaxCascades];
layout( set = 0, binding = 13 ) uniform sampler3D voxelZ[kMaxCascades];
layout( set = 0, binding = 14 ) uniform samplerCube skyCube;

/// The same ceiling the trace and the SSR loop hold their radiance to.
const float kMaxRadiance = 1024.0;
/// The alpha at or below which nothing is filtered (rq_reflect.comp's own
/// kMirrorAlpha: perceptual 0.1, where the reflected lobe first exceeds a pixel).
const float kMirrorAlpha = 0.01;
/// How many frames a glossy lobe warms up over before its answer is shown
/// (rq_reflect.comp's kWarmupFrames).
const float kWarmupFrames = 8.0;
/// THE KERNEL'S REACH. radius = kFilterScale * sqrt(alpha) = kFilterScale times
/// the PERCEPTUAL roughness, capped at kMaxRadius — so it saturates at
/// perceptual 0.30 and everything rougher than that shares the widest kernel. A
/// 7x7 neighbourhood at the trace resolution is ~49 samples of one lobe, which
/// is the variance reduction the temporal mean alone could only buy with
/// half a second of lag.
const float kFilterScale = 10.0;
const int   kMaxRadius = 3;
/// HOW FAR A TAP'S DEPTH MAY DEPART FROM THE PLANE THROUGH THE CENTRE, as a
/// multiple of how far the surface itself moves over that many texels (third
/// reader, item 4). The first form here was a flat 2 % of the distance, and it
/// was underived and wrong in the one case the fixtures never showed: a floor
/// seen from eye height at ten to twenty metres changes half a per cent to one
/// per cent PER PIXEL, so a 2 % window admits at most two rows and the kernel
/// collapses to a horizontal line — the filter silently stops filtering exactly
/// where a glossy floor needs it most.
///
/// This is SVGF's form (Schied et al. 2017): the tap is compared against the
/// depth the CENTRE'S OWN GRADIENT predicts for it, so a surface at any slope
/// is flat in this measure and only a real discontinuity — a silhouette — is
/// rejected. Three of the predicted change, plus a floor for a face-on plane
/// whose gradient is zero and whose only spread is the depth buffer's own
/// precision.
const float kDepthSigma = 3.0;
const float kDepthFloorRel = 0.002;
const float kNormalPower = 32.0;

bool finite1( float x ) { return ( floatBitsToUint( x ) & 0x7FFFFFFFu ) < 0x7F800000u; }
bool finite3( vec3 v ) { return finite1( v.x ) && finite1( v.y ) && finite1( v.z ); }

float viewDistance( float rawDepth )
{
	if( p.camPos.w < 0.5 )
		return abs( ( rawDepth - p.projParams.x ) / p.projParams.y );
	return p.projParams.y / ( rawDepth - p.projParams.x );
}

/// This trace texel's own full-resolution sample point, in uv.
vec2 traceUv( ivec2 tc, ivec2 scale, ivec2 fullRes )
{
	return ( vec2( tc * scale ) + 0.5 * vec2( scale ) ) / vec2( fullRes );
}

void main()
{
	const ivec2 traceRes = ivec2( p.resolution.xy );
	const ivec2 tc = ivec2( gl_GlobalInvocationID.xy );
	if( any( greaterThanEqual( tc, traceRes ) ) )
		return;

	const ivec2 fullRes = ivec2( p.resolution.zw );
	const ivec2 scale = max( fullRes / max( traceRes, ivec2( 1 ) ), ivec2( 1 ) );
	const ivec2 blockOrigin = tc * scale;
	const vec2 uv = traceUv( tc, scale, fullRes );
	// THE KERNEL MAY NOT CROSS THE SEAM BETWEEN THE EYES (lane REFLECT-VR-1).
	// Under stereo the two eyes are two images in one texture, and the columns
	// either side of the middle are the far edges of two different pictures: a
	// tap across them averages this eye's reflection with the other eye's, and
	// the depth and normal tests cannot reject it (the two eyes see the same
	// surfaces at nearly the same distance and orientation — that is what
	// stereo IS). So the tap range is the EYE'S half, not the texture's width.
	const int eye = eyeOfColumn( tc.x, traceRes.x );
	const int eyeX0 = p.stereo.x > 0.5 ? eye * ( traceRes.x / 2 ) : 0;
	const int eyeX1 = p.stereo.x > 0.5 ? eyeX0 + traceRes.x / 2 : traceRes.x;

	const float rawDepth = texture( sceneDepth, uv ).x;
	if( rawDepth <= 0.0 || rawDepth >= 1.0 )
		return;						// nothing was drawn here; the trace wrote nothing either

	const float roughGate = p.knobs.x;
	const float feather = p.projParams.w;
	// The SAME decode as the trace: ogre-patch 0043 packs the GGX ALPHA over
	// [0.001, 1], and the gate is stated in PERCEPTUAL roughness.
	const float lobeAlpha = max( texture( gBufShadowRoughness, uv ).y * 0.999 + 0.001, 1e-6 );
	const float roughness = sqrt( lobeAlpha );
	if( roughness > roughGate + feather )
		return;

	// WRITTEN THIS FRAME? THE DISTANCE IS THE FLAG (third reader, item 1). The
	// two history buffers PING-PONG, so `historyCur` at a texel the trace
	// declined this frame still holds the value from TWO frames ago — with a
	// live count — and compositing it would silently reverse the one thing the
	// decline is for: handing that pixel back to the probe. `historyDistCur` is
	// zeroed by every decline and written by every answer, so it is exactly the
	// "this texel has an answer this frame" bit, and it costs one r32 load.
	if( imageLoad( historyDistCur, tc ).x <= 0.0 )
		return;
	const vec4 centre = imageLoad( historyCur, tc );
	if( centre.w <= 0.0 )
		return;						// this texel has no answer to filter or composite

	vec3 mean = clamp( centre.xyz, vec3( 0.0 ), vec3( kMaxRadiance ) );
	float count = centre.w;

	// ---- THE FILTER ---------------------------------------------------------
	// A MIRROR SKIPS IT ENTIRELY, and not as an optimisation: blurring a mirror
	// destroys a correct image to remove a variance it does not have.
	// THE REACH IS AN ANGLE, SO IT IS A COUNT OF SCREEN PIXELS — AND A TRACE
	// TEXEL IS NOT ONE (round D). The kernel exists to span the lobe's own
	// angular width, and at half resolution one trace texel already covers two
	// screen pixels, so the same angle is HALF as many texels. Ignoring that
	// made the half-resolution row blur twice as far as the full-resolution one
	// for four times the relative cost — 0.222 ms against a 0.2 ms bar on the
	// saturated fixture, while full-res sat at 0.634 against 0.8.
	const float texelsPerPixel = max( float( scale.x ), 1.0 );
	const int radius = int( min( float( kMaxRadius ),
								 floor( kFilterScale * sqrt( max( lobeAlpha, 0.0 ) ) /
										texelsPerPixel ) ) );
	if( radius > 0 && lobeAlpha > kMirrorAlpha )
	{
		const float centreDist = viewDistance( rawDepth );
		const vec3 centreN = normalize( texture( gBufNormals, uv ).xyz * 2.0 - 1.0 );
		// THE DEPTH GRADIENT, per trace texel, by central difference. Four
		// depth taps once per pixel — not per tap — and they are what makes the
		// kernel slope-aware. A tap off the edge of the buffer contributes a
		// one-sided difference, which is what `clamp` below gives for free.
		vec2 grad = vec2( 0.0 );
		{
			// Clamped into THIS EYE's half (see eyeX0/eyeX1): a one-sided
			// difference at the seam, never a step into the other picture.
			const ivec2 lo = ivec2( eyeX0, 0 );
			const ivec2 lim = ivec2( eyeX1 - 1, traceRes.y - 1 );
			const ivec2 xr = clamp( tc + ivec2( 1, 0 ), lo, lim );
			const ivec2 xl = clamp( tc - ivec2( 1, 0 ), lo, lim );
			const ivec2 yu = clamp( tc + ivec2( 0, 1 ), lo, lim );
			const ivec2 yd = clamp( tc - ivec2( 0, 1 ), lo, lim );
			const float dxr = texture( sceneDepth, traceUv( xr, scale, fullRes ) ).x;
			const float dxl = texture( sceneDepth, traceUv( xl, scale, fullRes ) ).x;
			const float dyu = texture( sceneDepth, traceUv( yu, scale, fullRes ) ).x;
			const float dyd = texture( sceneDepth, traceUv( yd, scale, fullRes ) ).x;
			const float sx = float( max( xr.x - xl.x, 1 ) ), sy = float( max( yu.y - yd.y, 1 ) );
			// A neighbour that drew nothing contributes no gradient rather than
			// a gradient to the far plane.
			if( dxr > 0.0 && dxr < 1.0 && dxl > 0.0 && dxl < 1.0 )
				grad.x = ( viewDistance( dxr ) - viewDistance( dxl ) ) / sx;
			if( dyu > 0.0 && dyu < 1.0 && dyd > 0.0 && dyd < 1.0 )
				grad.y = ( viewDistance( dyu ) - viewDistance( dyd ) ) / sy;
		}
		vec3  sum = mean;
		float sumW = 1.0;
		float sumCount = count;
		for( int dy = -radius; dy <= radius; ++dy )
		{
			for( int dx = -radius; dx <= radius; ++dx )
			{
				if( dx == 0 && dy == 0 )
					continue;
				const ivec2 t = tc + ivec2( dx, dy );
				if( t.x < eyeX0 || t.x >= eyeX1 || t.y < 0 || t.y >= traceRes.y )
					continue;			// outside this EYE's own picture
				// The SAME flag for a tap, and for a sharper reason: without it
				// a neighbour that was traced before a dial drag took it above
				// the cutoff is averaged into this pixel FOR EVER, because
				// nothing ever writes over it again.
				const float tapDist = imageLoad( historyDistCur, t ).x;
				if( tapDist <= 0.0 )
					continue;
				const vec4 h = imageLoad( historyCur, t );
				if( h.w <= 0.0 || !finite3( h.xyz ) )
					continue;
				const vec2 tuv = traceUv( t, scale, fullRes );
				const float td = texture( sceneDepth, tuv ).x;
				if( td <= 0.0 || td >= 1.0 )
					continue;
				// DEPTH: against the plane the centre's own gradient predicts,
				// so a floor at a grazing angle is FLAT in this measure and only
				// a silhouette is rejected.
				const float tDist = viewDistance( td );
				const vec2  d2 = vec2( dx, dy );
				const float predicted = centreDist + dot( grad, d2 );
				const float tol = kDepthSigma * ( abs( dot( grad, d2 ) ) +
												  kDepthFloorRel * centreDist );
				const float dz = abs( tDist - predicted );
				if( dz > tol )
					continue;
				// ORIENTATION: two texels that face differently reflect
				// differently, however close they are.
				const vec3 tn = normalize( texture( gBufNormals, tuv ).xyz * 2.0 - 1.0 );
				const float ndot = max( dot( tn, centreN ), 0.0 );
				// SPATIAL: a plain tent over the kernel, which is what an
				// à-trous step of 1 is at this radius.
				const float sw = max( 0.0, 1.0 - length( vec2( dx, dy ) ) / float( radius + 1 ) );
				const float w = sw * pow( ndot, kNormalPower ) * ( 1.0 - dz / tol );
				if( w <= 0.0 )
					continue;
				sum += h.xyz * w;
				sumW += w;
				// The WARM-UP reads the neighbourhood too: a pixel whose own
				// history is young but whose neighbours are converged is, after
				// this filter, holding a converged answer.
				sumCount += h.w * w;
			}
		}
		mean = sum / sumW;
		count = sumCount / sumW;
	}
	if( !finite3( mean ) )
		return;

	// ---- THE WRITE, per full-resolution texel of this block ------------------
	// Every texel decides for ITSELF whether the ray answers for it: its own
	// roughness, its own screen confidence, its own depth. A half-resolution
	// trace must not hand its neighbours' pixels an answer their own march
	// already had, and must not paint a ray over the sky.
	for( int dy = 0; dy < scale.y; ++dy )
	{
		for( int dx = 0; dx < scale.x; ++dx )
		{
			const ivec2 fc = blockOrigin + ivec2( dx, dy );
			if( any( greaterThanEqual( fc, fullRes ) ) )
				continue;
			const vec2 fuv = ( vec2( fc ) + 0.5 ) / vec2( fullRes );
			const float fd = texture( sceneDepth, fuv ).x;
			if( fd <= 0.0 || fd >= 1.0 )
				continue;
			// PERCEPTUAL again, from the same 0043 packing (see the decode above):
			// this texel's own gate, in the same units as the dial.
			const float fr = sqrt( max( texture( gBufShadowRoughness, fuv ).y * 0.999 + 0.001,
										1e-6 ) );
			if( fr > roughGate + feather )
				continue;
			const vec4 screen = imageLoad( ssrReflection, fc );
			const float screenW = clamp( screen.w, 0.0, 1.0 );
			if( screenW >= 1.0 )
				continue;				// the march owns this pixel outright
			// THE FEATHER (owner, ledger §426). Full confidence up to
			// `cutoff - feather`, none at `cutoff + feather`, smooth between —
			// and what the ray gives up the probe takes through the SAME
			// confidence composite, so a floor whose roughness varies across it
			// crossfades instead of stepping.
			float gate =
				1.0 - smoothstep( roughGate - feather, roughGate + feather, fr );
			// THE RAY FADES IN AS THE MEAN CONVERGES (second reader, M2). A
			// GRADED SCREENSHOT's view lives about two frames, so a glossy
			// traced reflection in a user's shot would be one or two samples of
			// a lobe — visible noise in a picture that is supposed to be the
			// editor's own. Weighting the trace's confidence by how much of the
			// integral it actually holds hands those pixels back to the probe,
			// which is today's picture: noise-free, deterministic, and the
			// worst case is "the shot looks like the renderer without rays".
			//
			// It is the SAME physics as the blend above, so it is the same
			// term: a MIRROR has no variance and needs no warm-up (one sample
			// is the whole integral), and the warm-up's length grows with the
			// lobe. A live viewport passes it within a few frames and is
			// unaffected; a two-frame view never reaches it on a glossy
			// surface, which is the point.
			// FROM THE PACKING FLOOR (third reader, item 3): 0.001 is the
			// sharpest alpha the G-buffer can hold, so it IS a mirror and must
			// warm up instantly rather than to 97.2 %.
			const float warm = max( 1.0 - smoothstep( 0.001, kMirrorAlpha, lobeAlpha ),
									min( count * ( 1.0 / kWarmupFrames ), 1.0 ) );
			gate *= warm;
			if( gate <= 0.0 )
				continue;
			const vec3 rgb = mix( mean, screen.xyz, screenW );
			imageStore( ssrReflection, fc, vec4( rgb, mix( screenW, 1.0, gate ) ) );
		}
	}
}
